    },
    {
      "text": "we go back to the agent acts as the user, which is even worse",
      "timestamp_sec": 451,
      "why": "Takes a clear side against the impersonation pattern many teams ship."
    },
    {
      "text": "most of the MCP servers that we've worked with don't actually limit the tool context access to the agent based on which user authorized the agent",
      "timestamp_sec": 502,
      "why": "A specific, checkable indictment of current MCP server practice."
    },
    {
      "text": "the agent is still seeing the same surface regardless whom it is acting for",
      "timestamp_sec": 502,
      "why": "Names the failure mode in one line."
    },
    {
      "text": "the actor, in this case, an agent, has to be bound to the principal at all times. And the agent should have its own identity.",
      "timestamp_sec": 548,
      "why": "The central prescription."
    },
    {
      "text": "There's no extremely fine-grained scoping to say can this agent act at this hour? Can this agent read emails only from these senders? Can this agent send emails to only this recipients?",
      "timestamp_sec": 548,
      "why": "Makes the abstract demand for fine-grained scopes concrete with real examples."
    },
    {
      "text": "gone are the days when the broad scoped auth scopes that we defined is okay because in that case developer was writing a deterministic application and you can review the code",
      "timestamp_sec": 587,
      "why": "Ties the prescription back to the determinism argument."
    },
    {
      "text": "It should be at an attribute level scoping, it should be context level scoping, it should be principal level scoping.",
      "timestamp_sec": 628,
      "why": "The three axes of the proposed permission model."
    },
    {
      "text": "agents should be least privileged by default and they should be able to ask for just-in-time authorization if they want elevated scopes",
      "timestamp_sec": 628,
      "why": "A named, adoptable pattern."
    },
    {
      "text": "We have seen enough incidents where agents end up doing rogue things. They end up deleting production databases and stuff like that.",
      "timestamp_sec": 667,
      "why": "Grounds the risk claim in reported incidents rather than hypotheticals."
    },
    {
      "text": "you have to have absolute visibility into what your agent can do, every action that's taken in your system, who took it, on behalf of whom, and who authorized it, when was the authorization given, what authorization was given, how long is it given for",
      "timestamp_sec": 703,
      "why": "A complete audit requirements checklist in one sentence."
    },
    {
      "text": "if you can't deterministically control what your agent can or cannot do, then you're just praying that agent doesn't end up doing what it's not supposed to do. And praying is not a strategy, as we all know.",
      "timestamp_sec": 703,
      "why": "The talk's rhetorical payoff."
    },
    {
      "text": "OAuth is a good place to start, but you need something beyond OAuth to make sure that the agents have extremely fine-grained access controls",
      "timestamp_sec": 751,
      "why": "The closing position: OAuth necessary but insufficient."
    }
